,379,15170,59,37018,90,18,481,220,17,59,26888,90,18,3417,90,18,3417,284,481,59,37018,90,18,2075,488,379,9139,90,18,481,220,17,59,26888,90,18,11248,59,2533,1249,39721,1124,11520,37018,35702,9407,1124,7141,1124,9407,1124,19127,15170,59,9407,1124,32214,11035,701,582,990,279,9569,1124,11520,9407,1124,32214,284,481,59,9407,19788,7141,488,1124,19127,8,284,481,11520,9407,1124,7141,1124,9407,1124,19127,481,1124,15940,1124,7141,1124,15940,1124,19127,10699,568,4354,11,264,803,30339,5486,374,311,990,279,1985,4686,75811,39421,323,279,2661,1995,382,1654,1414,429,1447,59,9640,59,9407,1124,32214,284,481,59,9407,19788,7141,488,1124,19127,8,284,481,11520,9407,1124,7141,1124,9407,1124,19127,481,1124,15940,1124,7141,1124,15940,1124,19127,340,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ be three angles such that $\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi.$  If we are given that $\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\csc \\frac{\\pi}{3},$ then determine $\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the problem, we start by using the given information and trigonometric identities. We know that \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\), which implies \\(\\gamma = \\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)\\). Therefore, we can use the identity for the tangent of a sum of angles to express \\(\\tan \\gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = \\tan (\\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)) = -\\tan (\\alpha + \\beta) = -\\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta}\n\\]\n\nWe are also given that \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\csc \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\csc 60^\\circ = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\). Let \\(x = \\tan \\alpha\\) and \\(y = \\tan \\beta\\). Then \\(xy = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\). Using the tangent addition formula, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = -\\frac{x + y}{1 - xy} = -\\frac{x + y}{1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{x + y}{\\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{3(x + y)}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\), we use the identity \\(\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\\). However, a more straightforward approach is to use the product-to-sum identities and the given information.\n\nWe know that:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"02a113c72ebf67c8f0b1239685a1101c81d47d5fb954fea57b02c3cf71543b43"},"seconds":49.320767641067505,"seed":1028193855488935176,"stop":"eos","text":"To find \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\), we can use the identity for \\(\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gamma = \\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)\\), we have \\(\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\\). Substituting the expression for \\(\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this, we use the trigonometric identity \\(1 + \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta + \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta}{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta} = \\frac{1}{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}\\). Given \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n1 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\frac{3}{3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nNow, substituting back:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta)} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{1}{-(1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})} = \\frac{1}{-\\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{3}{3 - \\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3} - 3}\n\\]\n\nRationalizing the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3} - 3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 3}{\\sqrt{3} + 3} = \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 3)}{3 - 9} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 9}{-6} = -\\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 9}{6} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\), we can use the identity for \\(\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gamma = \\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)\\), we have \\(\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\\). Substituting the expression for \\(\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this, we use the trigonometric identity \\(1 + \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta + \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta}{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta} = \\frac{1}{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}\\). Given \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n1 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\frac{3}{3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nNow, substituting back:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta)} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{1}{-(1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})} = \\frac{1}{-\\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{3}{3 - \\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3} - 3}\n\\]\n\nRationalizing the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3} - 3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 3}{\\sqrt{3} + 3} = \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 3)}{3 - 9} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 9}{-6} = -\\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 9}{6} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:399"}
